shared_nvfp4_swiglu_qmv_rows1 (CHANGED)       281.2 ->  286.7   +1.94%

Every memory-heavy DECODE kernel is slightly faster in the pairwise arm while
the prefill-dominated gather-GEMM is unchanged and the changed kernel is
slower. That is not a global clock/thermal offset and it is not the intended
per-kernel effect. Two candidate explanations, which this experiment cannot
separate:
  (a) real decode-phase cache/bandwidth relief: the halved plane removes
      39 x 65,408 B = 2.55 MB of scale reads per decode step, which stops
      evicting the neighbours' working set; but 2.55 MB is only ~6-7 us of
      bandwidth time, far less than the ~59 us the neighbours gain, so the
      effect would have to be an amplified cache-residency effect; or
  (b) an allocation/layout artifact: the pairwise arm installs an extra
      65,664 B x 39 = 2.6 MB of resident planes at load, shifting every later
      buffer address, and it compiles and binds a second PSO
      (..._rows1_ps_bf16_v1).

Verdict: NOT a validated win. The mechanism as designed (fewer scale bytes in
the changed kernel) is refuted on this host at the kernel level (+1.94%,
CI excludes 0). The whole-step improvement is real in these 12 processes but is
not attributable to the mechanism, and (b) would not transfer to another host.
Stage 3 (uninstrumented, trusted harness) is the deciding measurement.
